MyGo patch 0004 (Auralink): Window.SetVibrancyStyle.

Auralink's recording pill is a transparent, borderless, non-activating
panel that draws a rounded HUD with room around it for its own shadow
(the window has none: macOS would shadow its rectangle). A blurred
material behind the HUD (what the user dictates over shows through,
frosted) cannot come from native UI's materials (plugins/glass blurs
what MyGo painted under an element, not the desktop): it takes the
window's NSVisualEffectView. SetVibrancy puts that view under the whole
content view, a rectangle that would show in the shadow's margin.

VibrancyStyle insets the material from the content's edges, rounds its
corners with a stretchable mask image (maskImage with cap insets, as
AppKit documents for shaped materials; CALayer corners do not clip a
behind-window blur everywhere), and can show it in the dark appearance
whatever the app's (a HUD that stays dark over light and dark content).
The effect view keeps its insets as the window resizes (autoresizing).
Other platforms ignore it: a Windows system backdrop fills the window.
